* Sat Sep 26 2026 Jeremy Melanson <1080872+zish@users.noreply.github.com> - 1.1.0-1
- Three new manifest row types, and the mechanism the no-vendored-binaries
  policy needs (docs/54). A DERIVE row carries no bytes: it records where the
  file lives inside the user's own system.img or vendor.img, the hash it must
  have, the hash the patched result must have, and the byte edits between them.
  The reconciler extracts with debugfs -- read-only and unprivileged, because
  the images are 0644 ext2 -- verifies, patches, verifies again, and installs.
- The refusal is the feature, not the fallback. A stock hash that has stopped
  matching means an image update has moved under a file a component silently
  replaces, so the reconciler installs nothing and says which file and which two
  hashes. One refused row does not abandon the rest of the overlay.
- A LINK row, for a symlink that is load-bearing rather than cosmetic. The
  format could not express one and the tool tested [ -f ] and ran install, so
  such a link was created by hand and owned by nothing (docs/47). deployed.list
  records it as link:<target> rather than as a hash, there being no contents to
  hash and following it hashing whatever it points at instead.
- --check-upstream, which the stock hashes have been recorded for since the day
  the format was written and which nothing implemented. It asks the derive
  question of every row that records an upstream hash, changes nothing, and
  exits 3 if any image file has moved.
- e2fsprogs is now a hard Requires. A component that ships a patch instead of a
  binary deploys nothing without debugfs, and would do so quietly.
- Proved rather than asserted: bin/overlay-sync-test.sh builds a real ext2 image
  with mke2fs -d, derives both patched HALs out of it, and asserts the results
  are byte-identical to the patched binaries this repository already carries.
  37 checks, no privilege, bigtab01 untouched.

* Wed Sep 23 2026 Jeremy Melanson <1080872+zish@users.noreply.github.com> - 1.0.0-1
- First package, and the one every overlay component has been waiting on: until
  now they declared Requires: waydroid-ext-overlay-sync against a package that
  had never been written, so all of them built cleanly and none could install.
- Owns the two staging directories as well as the tool, because component
  packages list only their own subdirectory and their own manifest.
- The uninstall scriptlet reports drift and leaves /var/lib/waydroid/overlay
  in place. The
  overlay is state, not installed content, and an uninstall that discarded it
  would be removing Android's working configuration to tidy up after itself.
